<section xml:id="sec-c-6-practice-problems">
  <title>Practice problems</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-unit-circle-definitions">
    <title>Unit circle definitions</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Convert <m>120^\circ</m> to radians.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Convert <m>\frac{11\pi}{6}</m> radians to degrees.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A circle has radius <m>5</m>.  Find the arc length cut off by an angle of
<m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m> radians.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A sector has radius <m>4</m> and central angle <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m>.  Find its area.
Use</p>
          <md>A=\frac12 r^2\theta</md>
        <p>for <m>\theta</m> in radians.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find <m>\sin\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m>, and <m>\tan\theta</m> for</p>
          <md>\theta=\frac{7\pi}{6}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find <m>\sin\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m>, and <m>\tan\theta</m> for</p>
          <md>\theta=\frac{3\pi}{4}.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-basic-identities">
    <title>Basic identities</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>If</p>
          <md>\cos\theta=-\frac35</md>
        <p>and <m>\theta</m> is in Quadrant III, find <m>\sin\theta</m> and <m>\tan\theta</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
          <md>1-\sin^2 x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
          <md>\frac{1-\cos^2 x}{\sin x},
\qquad \sin x\neq0.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
          <md>\sec^2 x-\tan^2 x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Verify the identity:</p>
          <md>(1-\cos x)(1+\cos x)=\sin^2 x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Simplify:</p>
          <md>\sin(-x)+\cos(-x).</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

