  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-unit-circle-definitions-2">
    <title>Unit circle definitions</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>120^\circ=120\cdot\frac{\pi}{180}=\frac{2\pi}{3}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{11\pi}{6}\cdot\frac{180}{\pi}=330^\circ.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>s=r\theta=5\cdot\frac{3\pi}{4}=\frac{15\pi}{4}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>A=\frac12 r^2\theta
=
\frac12(4^2)\frac{\pi}{3}
=
\frac{8\pi}{3}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The angle <m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m> is in Quadrant III with reference angle <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m>.
Thus</p>
          <md>\sin\frac{7\pi}{6}=-\frac12,
\qquad
\cos\frac{7\pi}{6}=-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},
\qquad
\tan\frac{7\pi}{6}=\frac{\sqrt3}{3}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The angle <m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m> is in Quadrant II with reference angle <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
Thus</p>
          <md>\sin\frac{3\pi}{4}=\frac{\sqrt2}{2},
\qquad
\cos\frac{3\pi}{4}=-\frac{\sqrt2}{2},
\qquad
\tan\frac{3\pi}{4}=-1.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-basic-identities-2">
    <title>Basic identities</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Since</p>
          <md>\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1,</md>
        <p>we get</p>
          <md>\sin^2\theta=1-\frac{9}{25}=\frac{16}{25}.</md>
        <p>In Quadrant III, sine is negative, so</p>
          <md>\sin\theta=-\frac45.</md>
        <p>Then</p>
          <md>\tan\theta=\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}
=
\frac{-4/5}{-3/5}
=
\frac43.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>1-\sin^2 x=\cos^2 x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{1-\cos^2 x}{\sin x}
=
\frac{\sin^2 x}{\sin x}
=
\sin x,
\qquad \sin x\neq0.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sec^2 x-\tan^2 x=1.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>(1-\cos x)(1+\cos x)=1-\cos^2 x=\sin^2 x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sin(-x)+\cos(-x)=-\sin x+\cos x.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-addition-formulas-2">
    <title>Addition formulas</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>\sin\left(x+\frac{\pi}{2}\right)
=
\sin x\cos\frac{\pi}{2}
+
\cos x\sin\frac{\pi}{2}
=
\cos x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\cos(x-\pi)
=
\cos x\cos\pi+\sin x\sin\pi
=
-\cos x.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\frac{5\pi}{12}=\frac{\pi}{4}+\frac{\pi}{6}.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right)
=
\cos\frac{\pi}{4}\cos\frac{\pi}{6}
-
\sin\frac{\pi}{4}\sin\frac{\pi}{6}.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>\cos\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right)
=
\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\cdot\frac{\sqrt3}{2}
-
\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\cdot\frac12
=
\frac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\cos^2 x=\frac{1+\cos(2x)}{2}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sin(5x)\cos(2x)
=
\frac12\bigl(\sin(7x)+\sin(3x)\bigr).</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\cos(4x)\cos x
=
\frac12\bigl(\cos(5x)+\cos(3x)\bigr).</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-inverse-trigonometric-functions-2">
    <title>Inverse trigonometric functions</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>\arcsin\left(-\frac12\right)=-\frac{\pi}{6}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\arccos\left(\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)=\frac{\pi}{4}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\arctan(\sqrt3)=\frac{\pi}{3}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Let</p>
          <md>\theta=\arccos x.</md>
        <p>Then <m>\cos\theta=x</m> and <m>0\le\theta\le\pi</m>.  On this interval, <m>\sin\theta\ge0</m>.
Thus</p>
          <md>\sin(\arccos x)=\sqrt{1-x^2}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Let</p>
          <md>\theta=\arctan 2.</md>
        <p>Then <m>\tan\theta=2=\frac{2}{1}</m>.  The hypotenuse is <m>\sqrt5</m>, so</p>
          <md>\cos(\arctan 2)=\frac1{\sqrt5}.</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Let</p>
          <md>\theta=\arcsin\frac35.</md>
        <p>Then <m>\sin\theta=\frac35</m>.  Since <m>\theta\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2]</m>, the cosine is
nonnegative:</p>
          <md>\cos\theta=\frac45.</md>
        <p>Therefore</p>
          <md>\tan\left(\arcsin\frac35\right)
=
\frac{3/5}{4/5}
=
\frac34.</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-c-6-practice-problems-trigonometric-equations-2">
    <title>Trigonometric equations</title>

    <ol>
      <li>
          <md>\sin x=\frac12</md>
        <p>on <m>0\le x&lt;2\pi</m> gives</p>
          <md>
x=\frac{\pi}{6},\ \frac{5\pi}{6}.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\cos x=-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</md>
        <p>on <m>0\le x&lt;2\pi</m> gives</p>
          <md>
x=\frac{5\pi}{6},\ \frac{7\pi}{6}.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\tan x=-1</md>
        <p>on <m>0\le x&lt;2\pi</m> gives</p>
          <md>
x=\frac{3\pi}{4},\ \frac{7\pi}{4}.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>2\sin^2 x-\sin x=0</md>
        <p>factors as</p>
          <md>\sin x(2\sin x-1)=0.</md>
        <p>So</p>
          <md>\sin x=0
\quad\text{or}\quad
\sin x=\frac12.</md>
        <p>On <m>0\le x&lt;2\pi</m>,</p>
          <md>
x=0,\ \frac{\pi}{6},\ \frac{5\pi}{6},\ \pi.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For</p>
          <md>\cos(2x)=-\frac12,</md>
        <p>with <m>0\le x&lt;2\pi</m>, we have <m>0\le2x&lt;4\pi</m>.  Cosine equals <m>-1/2</m> at</p>
          <md>2x=\frac{2\pi}{3},\ \frac{4\pi}{3},\ \frac{8\pi}{3},\ \frac{10\pi}{3}.</md>
        <p>Divide by <m>2</m>:</p>
          <md>
x=\frac{\pi}{3},\ \frac{2\pi}{3},\ \frac{4\pi}{3},\ \frac{5\pi}{3}.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sec x=2</md>
        <p>means</p>
          <md>\cos x=\frac12.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>
x=\frac{\pi}{3}+2\pi k
\quad\text{or}\quad
x=\frac{5\pi}{3}+2\pi k,
\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.
</md>
      </li>
      <li>
          <md>\sin x=\cos x</md>
        <p>gives</p>
          <md>\tan x=1.</md>
        <p>Thus</p>
          <md>
x=\frac{\pi}{4}+\pi k,
\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.
</md>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

